     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day, which is double the usual maximum. The FDA-labeled maximum is 2,550 mg per day for immediate-release metformin, usually given as 850 mg three times daily. A dose this high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThe dose may be a typo. Some possibilities:\n- **850 mg three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg per day and the maximum.\n- **1000 mg twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg per day.\n- **500 mg to 1000 mg** at some frequency, depending on the patient's current regimen.\n\nThe order should come from the prescribing clinician, or be confirmed by them. They can tell me the intended dose, or enter the order themselves. If the high dose is intentional, they should say why, and I can enter it with a note, though pharmacy will probably want to verify it.\n\nOnce I have a confirmed dose, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
